 "note": [
  "Each chapter's KJV epigraph is replaced by Louis Segond (1910) verbatim (only French typographic spacing added) and the “(KJV)” label is dropped, as in a-retrospect-teens.fr. Partial-verse epigraphs (1 Peter 1:5, Mark 9:24, Luke 18:27, Luke 22:61–62, Romans 7:24–25a) quote the matching Segond span; Galatians 5:22 quotes the whole Segond verse, whose list runs on to « la douceur, la tempérance » (KJV puts those in v. 23). Segond's Mark 9:24 has no « Seigneur », so the KJV's “Lord” drops out in ch. 4 (epigraph and body).",
  "Murray's own sentences are pasted from the shipped absolute-surrender.fr.json (« L’abandon absolu ») and keep its « vous » even though the teen prose says « tu ». One deliberate change: Müller's “lover of God’s Word” is « un amoureux de la Parole de Dieu », not the full edition's « un amant », which reads as romantic to a French teenager. Hampton Court's “hundreds of yards” follows the full edition's « centaines de mètres ».",
  "Term choices: “surrender” = « abandon / s’abandonner » throughout, so “give up” is « renoncer / baisser les bras », never « abandonner ». “Temper” is « l’humeur » inside Murray's clock quote (as in the full edition) and « colères / s’emporter » in the teen prose (ch. 6 title « Ce que révèlent tes colères »). “Pen” is « plume » everywhere to match Murray's quote. Peter's “I do not know the man” is Segond Matthew 26:72.",
  "Title « L’abandon absolu (Pour ados) » (28 chars) follows the full French edition's title. AI-translated in-session; awaiting native review."
 ],
